 As RFC 6902 sections 3 and 5 describe, each operation is validated when it is reached and evaluation
 stops at the first error, so `index` is that of the first operation, in order, that is malformed or
 fails. On failure nothing is returned: a patch applies completely or not at all. See [ADR 0002](docs/adr/0002-json-patch.md) for the design decisions.
+
+### JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a JSON Merge Patch to `target` and returns the merged
+document as a new value. Neither argument is mutated, and the result shares no object or array with
+them.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+applyMergePatch(
+  { title: "Goodbye!", author: { givenName: "John", familyName: "Doe" }, tags: ["example", "sample"] },
+  { title: "Hello!", author: { familyName: null }, tags: ["example"] },
+); // { title: "Hello!", author: { givenName: "John" }, tags: ["example"] }
+```
+
+An object patch changes only the members it names: a `null` member removes the target member (a
+missing one is ignored), an object member is merged recursively into the target member, and any other
+member, including an array, replaces it. A patch that is not an object (an array, a scalar or `null`)
+replaces the whole target. Merge patches therefore cannot set a member to `null` or change part of an
+array; use JSON Patch for that.
+
+It throws a `JsonMergePatchError` (whose `cause` holds the underlying error) when the target or the
+patch is not a JSON value: only `null`, booleans, finite numbers, strings, arrays and plain objects
+are. See [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-json-merge-patch.md) for the design decisions.
